1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:03,430
Léa: Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French!

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:04,800
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,400
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,820
Thank you for being with us,

5
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:09,740
whether you're discovering us today

6
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,000
or you come back every week.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,200
Léa: Get comfortable.

8
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,600
Today, we're going to talk about self-confidence.

9
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,960
Mathieu: Funny you say that, I'm waiting for my certificate.

10
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:24,050
As soon as I'm officially confident enough,

11
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:26,400
I'll be able to try new things.

12
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,440
Léa: And who's supposed to give it to you?

13
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,200
Mathieu: I'm still looking for the right department.

14
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,400
For now, nobody's answering.

15
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:34,150
Léa: Maybe that's

16
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:36,400
because you can start without that document.

17
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,950
Mathieu: Start before I feel completely ready?

18
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,820
That's an interesting proposal,

19
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:44,800
but slightly worrying.

20
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:46,030
Léa: We'll talk about it

21
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:48,400
with two very ordinary stories:

22
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,440
a neighbourhood meeting and a games night.

23
00:00:51,490 --> 00:00:55,490
Mathieu: This conversation is for levels B1 and B2.

24
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:57,260
We'll speak slowly

25
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:00,000
and explain useful expressions.

26
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,790
Léa: By the end, you'll be able to talk about your doubts,

27
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:06,400
recognise your strengths and ask for help.

28
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,120
Mathieu: You'll also be able to tell

29
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:11,000
a little experience that encouraged you.

30
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,180
Léa: No need to understand every word.

31
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:14,480
Follow the conversation,

32
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,000
then repeat a few sentences with us.

33
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,260
Mathieu: And you don't need

34
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:21,040
to speak perfect French to take part.

35
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:23,250
Léa: We're going to look precisely

36
00:01:23,250 --> 00:01:26,000
at what you can do when you're still hesitating.

37
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:26,890
Mathieu: Very well.

38
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:30,000
I'll leave my application form

39
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
for the certificate on the table.

40
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:32,930
Léa: Think of something

41
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:34,870
that seemed difficult at first

42
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:37,600
and that you do more easily now.

43
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,400
Mathieu: Ordering at a restaurant,

44
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,000
taking the metro in a new city

45
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
or asking a question during a class.

46
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,490
Léa: What changed?

47
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:49,640
Keep your example for our final exercise.

48
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:53,400
Mathieu: You can also tell us where you're listening to this episode from.

49
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:54,820
It's lovely to know

50
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,400
where our conversations go with you.

51
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,820
Léa: Last week,

52
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,410
I went to a meeting

53
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:02,800
to prepare the neighbourhood party.

54
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,600
I had drawn a poster idea.

55
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,150
Mathieu: Did you bring it with you?

56
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,000
Léa: Yes, in a big blue folder.

57
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,070
But when I arrived,

58
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:14,040
I felt intimidated by the other participants.

59
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:17,850
Mathieu: They impressed you and you felt less at ease.

60
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:20,200
That's what "être intimidé" means.

61
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,470
Léa: Exactly. For example,

62
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:24,290
my cousin felt intimidated

63
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:27,000
during his first rehearsal with the orchestra.

64
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,550
Mathieu: What impressed you about these people?

65
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,530
Léa: They already knew the place,

66
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:34,200
the schedules and the habits of the neighbourhood.

67
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,200
Me, I was taking part for the first time.

68
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,440
Mathieu: And your folder stayed closed?

69
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:40,440
Léa: Firmly closed.

70
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,760
Then the person in charge asked

71
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:45,800
if anyone had an idea for the poster.

72
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,400
I decided to dare to show my drawing.

73
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:48,960
Mathieu: "Oser"...

74
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:54,200
means doing something despite hesitation or some fear.

75
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,690
Léa: Yes. For example,

76
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:59,800
she dared to ask for another time for her interview.

77
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,350
Mathieu: How did you start?

78
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,790
Léa: I said: "I've prepared a first idea.

79
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:06,550
May I show it to you?"

80
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,640
Then I opened the folder.

81
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:11,400
Mathieu: So you had a real proposal.

82
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:12,870
It wasn't just

83
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:15,200
a very professional-looking folder.

84
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,400
Léa: Still, I wondered if I was up to it.

85
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,640
Mathieu: "Être à la hauteur"...

86
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:22,490
means having the necessary abilities

87
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:25,200
to meet what a situation requires.

88
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,200
Léa: That's it. For example, for this assignment,

89
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,200
our new colleague was up to the task:

90
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:33,600
she found a suitable solution.

91
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,840
Mathieu: And how did the others react?

92
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:38,400
Léa: One person liked the colours.

93
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,630
Another asked me where I'd put the date,

94
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:43,800
because it was hard to read.

95
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,600
Mathieu: A concrete question about your poster.

96
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,000
Léa: Yes. I suggested moving the title.

97
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,730
We looked at it together,

98
00:03:51,730 --> 00:03:54,600
and the layout became clearer.

99
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:56,550
Mathieu: Were you still a bit nervous?

100
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:59,200
Léa: Yes, but I could answer.

101
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,440
On the way home, I realised I had underestimated myself.

102
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:03,560
Mathieu: "Se sous-estimer"...

103
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,450
means thinking your abilities

104
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:08,800
are weaker than they really are.

105
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:09,800
Léa: For example,

106
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,940
you underestimate yourself when you say

107
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:14,250
you can't cook anything:

108
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:17,350
your gratin is excellent.

109
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:20,770
Mathieu: Thanks. I'll write down that compliment.

110
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:25,440
It can serve as supporting evidence for my certificate.

111
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:27,360
Léa: Above all, you can remind yourself

112
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,490
that you already know how to make

113
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:32,400
something other people enjoy.

114
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:35,200
Mathieu: My story takes place at the community centre.

115
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,320
I'd been asked to explain

116
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:40,000
a game to a few new participants.

117
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,950
Léa: A game you knew?

118
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,400
Mathieu: Very well. I'd played it several times.

119
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,540
Yet, in front of the six people sitting at the table,

120
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:51,030
I had stage fright.

121
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:52,100
Léa: "Avoir le trac"...

122
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:55,940
means feeling nervous before speaking,

123
00:04:55,940 --> 00:05:00,800
playing or doing something in front of other people.

124
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:01,880
Mathieu: For example,

125
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:03,050
my sister gets stage fright

126
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:05,800
before singing with her choir.

127
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,950
Léa: And you, had you prepared your explanation?

128
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,800
Mathieu: In my head, yes.

129
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:13,200
I started with a complicated rule,

130
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,500
then I went back to the beginning.

131
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,040
I was getting flustered.

132
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:19,480
Léa: "Perdre ses moyens"...

133
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:24,000
means no longer managing to use your usual abilities

134
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
because you're shaken or overwhelmed.

135
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,670
Mathieu: Another example: during the audition,

136
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:32,040
he got flustered

137
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:35,000
and forgot a passage he knew.

138
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,950
Léa: Did someone help you get going again?

139
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,080
Mathieu: A participant asked me:

140
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,040
"Can we watch one round of the game?"

141
00:05:42,090 --> 00:05:46,040
I showed the cards and played an example.

142
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:49,000
Léa: You used another way of explaining.

143
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Mathieu: Yes. Playing and explaining are two different skills.

144
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
I had one, but I was still discovering the other.

145
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,450
Léa: "Une compétence"...

146
00:05:59,450 --> 00:06:05,000
is an ability you can use to carry out a task.

147
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:05,910
Mathieu: For example,

148
00:06:05,910 --> 00:06:08,950
knowing how to organise orders is one

149
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:12,000
of my cousin's skills in his shop.

150
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,040
Léa: And after your demonstration, did the participants understand?

151
00:06:15,090 --> 00:06:17,020
Mathieu: Much better.

152
00:06:17,070 --> 00:06:19,940
Then someone asked what happened

153
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:23,000
if two players had the same score.

154
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,950
Léa: Did you know the answer?

155
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Mathieu: I didn't remember exactly.

156
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,090
I acknowledged my limits:

157
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:33,000
"For this case, I'd rather check the rulebook."

158
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,930
Léa: "Reconnaître ses limites"...

159
00:06:34,930 --> 00:06:36,530
means accepting and saying

160
00:06:36,530 --> 00:06:38,440
what you can't do

161
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:41,040
or what you don't know yet.

162
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:44,030
Mathieu: For example, to translate this contract,

163
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:46,000
she acknowledged her limits

164
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
and asked a specialist for help.

165
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,950
Léa: And the game went on.

166
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,960
Mathieu: Yes. The rulebook had the answer.

167
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:57,000
It was well prepared, at least.

168
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,280
Léa: What touches me in your story

169
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:03,040
is that your hesitation doesn't sum up the whole evening.

170
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:05,390
Mathieu: Still, on the bus home,

171
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:08,730
I was mostly thinking about my first explanation.

172
00:07:08,730 --> 00:07:13,000
I'd forgotten the parts that had gone well.

173
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Léa: You needed to step back.

174
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,300
Mathieu: "Prendre du recul"...

175
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:19,940
means looking at a situation from a little distance,

176
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:22,000
to understand it better.

177
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,090
Léa: Yes. For example, after a difficult discussion,

178
00:07:26,090 --> 00:07:28,000
we waited until the next day

179
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,020
to step back before deciding.

180
00:07:32,070 --> 00:07:35,000
Mathieu: In the end, I thought back to the participant.

181
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,010
She had suggested a demonstration and I'd done it.

182
00:07:39,010 --> 00:07:42,000
That was part of the story too.

183
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,510
Léa: Me, I mostly remembered

184
00:07:44,510 --> 00:07:48,000
the date being too small on my poster.

185
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
But I'd also found a solution with the group.

186
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,430
Mathieu: So you can question yourself

187
00:07:54,430 --> 00:07:57,040
without erasing everything you got right.

188
00:07:57,090 --> 00:07:58,970
Léa: "Se remettre en question"...

189
00:07:58,970 --> 00:08:01,590
means examining the way you do things

190
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:05,000
and accepting to change it if necessary.

191
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,430
Mathieu: For example, after the customers' comments,

192
00:08:08,430 --> 00:08:11,070
the restaurant owner reassessed his approach

193
00:08:11,070 --> 00:08:13,000
and simplified his menu.

194
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,480
Léa: For my poster, I could tell myself:

195
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:17,080
"Next time,

196
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:21,030
I'll check the size of the important information."

197
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,310
Mathieu: That's precise. And for my game:

198
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:26,030
"I'll start by showing a round,

199
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:29,000
then explain the special cases."

200
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Léa: That gives us something to try.

201
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,030
We don't need to decide

202
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:37,000
whether we're permanently good or bad.

203
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Mathieu: And you can stay careful when you don't know.

204
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,910
I wouldn't want to confuse

205
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:44,040
my enthusiasm with expertise.

206
00:08:44,090 --> 00:08:47,480
Léa: Especially if someone is counting on your answer

207
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:51,000
to make an important decision.

208
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,060
Mathieu: So my certificate will need a little box:

209
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:58,000
"Knows how to ask for a check."

210
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Let's do a role play.

211
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,090
You arrive at a guided walk in the neighbourhood.

212
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:04,000
I'm a member of the group.

213
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
Léa: You'll get to try my first line before me.

214
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,030
Mathieu: Hello! Are you coming walking with us today?

215
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:14,000
Léa: Your turn! It's your first visit.

216
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,040
How would you ask if you're in the right place?

217
00:09:18,090 --> 00:09:20,000
Take a few seconds.

218
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,070
Mathieu: You can start with:

219
00:09:22,070 --> 00:09:25,000
"Hello, is this where…?"

220
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,550
Léa: Hello, is this where

221
00:09:26,550 --> 00:09:29,010
the walking group meets?

222
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,000
It's my first time coming.

223
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Mathieu: Yes, welcome! I'm Mathieu.

224
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,040
We're still waiting for two people before leaving.

225
00:09:38,090 --> 00:09:41,000
Léa: Nice to meet you, I'm Léa.

226
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
I saw your ad and decided to take the plunge.

227
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:44,860
Mathieu: "Se lancer"...

228
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:47,090
means starting something

229
00:09:47,090 --> 00:09:48,880
you were hesitating to do

230
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:51,000
or that is new to you.

231
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Léa: For example, after several tries at home,

232
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
my friend took the plunge

233
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
and showed her drawings at the village fair.

234
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Mathieu: Very good. Back to the walk.

235
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,040
Do you already know the neighbourhood a bit?

236
00:10:04,090 --> 00:10:05,920
Léa: Yes, but not the route.

237
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:08,950
Did you say we're heading to the canal?

238
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I didn't hear properly.

239
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,640
Mathieu: Yes, towards the canal,

240
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:16,000
then to the little garden near the bridge.

241
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,950
Léa: Okay, thanks. Do we come back here at the end?

242
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,240
Mathieu: Exactly. And you can walk

243
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:24,000
next to me if you like.

244
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,950
Léa: With pleasure!

245
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,620
Mathieu: There you go. You started the conversation,

246
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:32,040
asked for clarification and accepted an offer.

247
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:35,000
Léa: And I was able to say I hadn't heard.

248
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
You can reuse that sentence

249
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
in a real conversation in French.

250
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,090
Mathieu: A simple question often keeps the conversation going.

251
00:10:44,090 --> 00:10:48,000
No need to come up with a spectacular introduction.

252
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,080
Léa: When I think back to the meeting,

253
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:53,440
I now know I was able to present an idea

254
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:56,040
and discuss a change.

255
00:10:56,090 --> 00:10:58,610
Mathieu: You had the chance to prove yourself.

256
00:10:58,610 --> 00:10:59,910
Léa: "Faire ses preuves"...

257
00:10:59,910 --> 00:11:03,000
means showing your abilities through what you do.

258
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,230
Example: during her internship,

259
00:11:05,230 --> 00:11:09,000
she proved herself by preparing the visits.

260
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,470
Mathieu: But that doesn't mean

261
00:11:10,470 --> 00:11:13,090
taking an exam in every conversation.

262
00:11:13,090 --> 00:11:17,040
I can also try something just to learn.

263
00:11:17,090 --> 00:11:17,740
Léa: Of course.

264
00:11:17,740 --> 00:11:22,080
For my next meeting, I'll prepare two sentences:

265
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,500
what I'm proposing

266
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:27,000
and the question I'd like an opinion on.

267
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,390
Mathieu: Me, I'll rehearse a demonstration

268
00:11:29,390 --> 00:11:32,000
with a friend before the next games night.

269
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
I can ask him what's still unclear.

270
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:35,980
Léa: That can help you

271
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:39,000
grow more confident in that situation.

272
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,570
Mathieu: "Gagner en assurance"...

273
00:11:40,570 --> 00:11:42,470
means becoming more at ease

274
00:11:42,470 --> 00:11:45,040
and surer of the way you act.

275
00:11:45,090 --> 00:11:47,440
Léa: For example, over the tours,

276
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:50,140
the new guide grew more confident

277
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:52,000
speaking to the group.

278
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,090
Mathieu: So I can choose a fairly small next try.

279
00:11:56,090 --> 00:11:58,030
Not organise a whole tournament tomorrow

280
00:11:58,030 --> 00:12:00,000
to prove I've understood.

281
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,030
Léa: You can already explain the first cards.

282
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:04,020
And afterwards,

283
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:05,990
note one thing that worked

284
00:12:05,990 --> 00:12:08,030
and another to clarify.

285
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:09,750
Mathieu: So having self-confidence

286
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:12,090
would mean believing you can

287
00:12:12,090 --> 00:12:14,840
act or learn in a situation,

288
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:17,000
even without mastering everything.

289
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,230
Léa: Yes. For example,

290
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:21,910
she feels confident welcoming visitors,

291
00:12:21,910 --> 00:12:23,500
while asking for help

292
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:26,000
with certain technical questions.

293
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,420
Mathieu: I like that idea.

294
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:31,000
My confidence can be about one specific activity.

295
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,940
I can be at ease in the kitchen

296
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:35,040
and hesitate in front of a group.

297
00:12:35,090 --> 00:12:38,000
Léa: And you can ask for conditions that help you:

298
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
a little time to prepare,

299
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
written instructions or a colleague being there.

300
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,810
Mathieu: If a try goes badly,

301
00:12:45,810 --> 00:12:48,690
I can also choose another moment

302
00:12:48,690 --> 00:12:51,000
or another way of doing it.

303
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,090
Léa: Exactly. We're talking about our ordinary experiences.

304
00:12:55,090 --> 00:12:57,450
Nobody has to put themselves

305
00:12:57,450 --> 00:13:00,040
in a situation that scares them too much.

306
00:13:00,090 --> 00:13:02,430
Mathieu: I'll start with my friend.

307
00:13:02,430 --> 00:13:04,940
He understands the rules very well

308
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:07,000
when there's cake left.

309
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
Léa: Go back to your example from the start.

310
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:14,080
Here's a structure: "Before, I hesitated to…

311
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:17,000
Now, I can…

312
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
I'm still learning to…"

313
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Mathieu: Before, I hesitated to explain a game to a group.

314
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,040
Now, I can show a round.

315
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:35,000
I'm still learning to answer clearly.

316
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Léa: Before, I hesitated to present my ideas.

317
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
Now, I can show a first proposal.

318
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,660
I'm still

319
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:47,000
learning to welcome questions.

320
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:48,080
Mathieu: Your turn.

321
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:50,600
Say your sentences out loud.

322
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:55,040
Your example can be very simple and very personal.

323
00:13:55,090 --> 00:13:59,090
Léa: You can also talk about a next try:

324
00:13:59,090 --> 00:14:02,000
"Next time, I'll try to…"

325
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,450
Mathieu: Next time,

326
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:07,000
I'll try to give a short instruction,

327
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
then let the participants play.

328
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,430
Léa: Me, I'll try to speak

329
00:14:12,430 --> 00:14:17,040
before preparing every possible answer.

330
00:14:17,090 --> 00:14:19,430
Mathieu: Choose what suits you.

331
00:14:19,430 --> 00:14:21,940
And if you're missing a word in French,

332
00:14:21,940 --> 00:14:25,000
you can describe your idea another way.

333
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Léa: Let's go back over five expressions.

334
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Listen to their meaning, then repeat with us.

335
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,090
Mathieu: First, "avoir le trac".

336
00:14:36,090 --> 00:14:37,440
Before my demonstration,

337
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:40,040
I was nervous in front of the participants.

338
00:14:40,090 --> 00:14:45,250
Léa: It's feeling that nervousness before a performance.

339
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:47,150
New example:

340
00:14:47,150 --> 00:14:48,000
she gets stage fright

341
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
before reading her text at the poetry evening.

342
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,090
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "avoir le trac".

343
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:56,440
You can say:

344
00:14:56,440 --> 00:15:00,040
"I'm a little nervous, it's my first time."

345
00:15:00,090 --> 00:15:03,430
Léa: Second expression: "être à la hauteur".

346
00:15:03,430 --> 00:15:05,030
During the meeting,

347
00:15:05,030 --> 00:15:07,130
I doubted my ability

348
00:15:07,130 --> 00:15:10,000
to propose a suitable poster.

349
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Mathieu: It's being able to meet what the situation requires.

350
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,060
Example: our team rose to the occasion

351
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:21,040
despite the change of plan.

352
00:15:21,090 --> 00:15:24,680
Léa: Let's repeat: "être à la hauteur".

353
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:30,000
You can specify: "up to the task".

354
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,090
Mathieu: Third expression: "prendre du recul".

355
00:15:34,090 --> 00:15:39,000
On the bus, I was only thinking about my difficult start.

356
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,090
Léa: It's looking at the situation from a distance.

357
00:15:43,090 --> 00:15:46,870
Example: before replying to that comment,

358
00:15:46,870 --> 00:15:49,040
I'll take a step back.

359
00:15:49,090 --> 00:15:51,390
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "prendre du recul".

360
00:15:51,390 --> 00:15:54,650
We can ask ourselves what happened,

361
00:15:54,650 --> 00:15:57,000
with concrete details.

362
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,020
Léa: Fourth expression: "se lancer".

363
00:16:00,020 --> 00:16:02,100
In our little scene,

364
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:06,000
I decided to join the walking group.

365
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:10,090
Mathieu: It's starting despite some hesitation.

366
00:16:10,090 --> 00:16:13,350
Example: after finding a teacher,

367
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:17,040
he took the plunge and started learning the piano.

368
00:16:17,090 --> 00:16:18,890
Léa: Let's repeat: "se lancer".

369
00:16:18,890 --> 00:16:21,270
Your next try can be

370
00:16:21,270 --> 00:16:25,000
a simple question in a conversation.

371
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,010
Mathieu: Finally, "gagner en assurance".

372
00:16:28,010 --> 00:16:30,220
With experience,

373
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:35,000
you can become more at ease in an activity.

374
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,200
Léa: Example: since she started presenting the school weather report,

375
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:43,040
this pupil has grown more confident in front of the camera.

376
00:16:43,090 --> 00:16:47,080
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "gagner en assurance".

377
00:16:47,080 --> 00:16:49,600
Now think of an experience

378
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:53,000
where you noticed a small change.

379
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,520
Léa: Choose one of these expressions

380
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:59,000
and tell that moment in your own words.

381
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Mathieu: Even with some hesitation.

382
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
You can ask us to repeat;

383
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,040
we know that sentence very well.

384
00:17:08,090 --> 00:17:14,000
Léa: In the comments, complete:

385
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,360
"I'd like to feel more at ease when…

386
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:17,000
Next time, I'll try to…"

387
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,590
Mathieu: You can also tell us something

388
00:17:19,590 --> 00:17:21,940
you can do today

389
00:17:21,940 --> 00:17:25,000
that used to seem difficult.

390
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,170
Léa: If this episode helped you,

391
00:17:27,170 --> 00:17:29,930
subscribe to The Cozy French

392
00:17:29,930 --> 00:17:33,030
to keep practising with us.

393
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:36,520
Mathieu: Listen again to a few lines from the walking group,

394
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:38,200
then change the details

395
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:41,000
for an activity that interests you.

396
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,300
Thank you for sharing this moment.

397
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:46,000
Take care and see you very soon!

398
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Léa: Goodbye everyone!

399
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Mathieu: Right, I need to cancel my certificate application.

400
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:52,950
Léa: Why?

401
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,770
Mathieu: I've already explained a game

402
00:17:54,770 --> 00:17:58,000
and welcomed a new participant.

403
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,040
I didn't wait for it to be printed.

404
00:18:01,090 --> 00:18:01,710
Léa: Very good.

405
00:18:01,710 --> 00:18:03,770
Mathieu: You can start your next try

406
00:18:03,770 --> 00:18:07,000
while the printer takes a rest.
